    `The grace received on Monday was never meant to remain a private possession. It was always meant to flow outward — and Saturday brings the week full circle by addressing the most concrete and often the most difficult expression of that outward flow: forgiveness. Ephesians 4:32 gives the standard with a clarity that leaves no room for negotiation: forgiving each other, just as in Christ God forgave you. The measure of the forgiveness you are called to extend is not the size of the offence against you. It is the size of the forgiveness you yourself have already received — which, if Monday's reflection on grace was genuinely absorbed, is far larger than almost any offence another person could commit against you. Peter's question in Matthew 18:21 reveals how most people instinctively think about forgiveness — as a finite resource with a reasonable limit. Up to seven times? Peter is being generous by the standards of his culture, and he likely expected to be commended for the offer. Jesus's response — not seven times, but seventy-seven times — is not a new, larger finite number to count toward. It is a deliberate rejection of the counting mentality altogether. The number is symbolic of completeness, of forgiveness without a ledger, of a posture that does not keep score because keeping score was never the point. This is illustrated immediately afterward in the parable of the unforgiving servant — a man forgiven an impossibly large debt who then refuses to forgive a small debt owed to him. The disproportion in the parable is the entire point. Most grievances held against another person, measured honestly against what God has already forgiven in us, are the small debt next to the impossibly large one. This does not minimise real pain — forgiveness is not a denial that genuine harm occurred. It is the decision to release the claim for repayment, trusting God with the justice of the situation rather than insisting on extracting it yourself. Colossians 3:13 gives the standard one more time, in case it was missed the first time: forgive as the Lord forgave you. Not partially. Not conditionally. Not only once the other person has sufficiently apologised or demonstrated change. As the Lord forgave you — which, per Monday's reflection, happened while you were still a sinner, before any qualifying had taken place. This is the hardest and most freeing instruction in the entire week, because it asks you to extend to others the exact same unearned, unconditional grace that you received and that made this whole week's foundation possible in the first place.`
